  "body": {
    "id": "thm:verified-no-penalty-region",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\ge\\gamma\\) and \\(d\\le4\\gamma\\), then constants \\(0<c<C<\\infty\\) and \\(n_0\\), depending only on \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,d,L\\), satisfy \\(c r_{\\mathrm{or}}(n)\\le\\rho_n^*\\le C r_{\\mathrm{or}}(n)\\) for every \\(n\\ge n_0\\). In particular, \\(d=\\gamma=1\\), \\(\\alpha=1/10\\), and \\(\\beta=9/10\\) yield \\(\\rho_n^*\\asymp n^{-2/5}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "gamma",
      "d",
      "L",
      "rOracle",
      "n",
      "rhoStar"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:known-propensity-benchmark",
      "prop:single-scale-upper",
      "def:observational-critical-radius"
    ],
    "justification": "The asymmetric example proves that a first-order nuisance product condition is not the operative no-penalty mechanism.",
    "gap": "Hahn1998 proves propensity ancillarity for regular ATE efficiency; no prior result gives this nonregular testing equality region.",
    "consumer": "The theorem identifies a broad regime where an observational implementation pays no rate cost relative to receiving the true assignment law.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\ge\\gamma\\) and \\(d\\le4\\gamma\\). Then the exponent in \\(\\texttt{prop:single-scale-upper}\\) uses \\(t=\\min\\{\\gamma,\\alpha+\\beta\\}=\\gamma\\) and its dense case applies. The explicit test proved there and the definition of \\(\\rho_n^*\\) give\n\\[\n \\rho_n^*\\le C n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}=C r_{\\mathrm{or}}(n).        \\tag{31}\n\\]\nConversely, every observational test \\(\\phi(O^n)\\) induces a known-propensity test \\((O^n,e_P)\\mapsto\\phi(O^n)\\) by ignoring the supplied function. Its rejection probabilities are unchanged on every law in the common null and alternative classes. Hence the feasible set of known-propensity tests contains the observational feasible set and\n\\[\n \\rho_{n,e}^*\\le\\rho_n^*.                                           \\tag{32}\n\\]\nThe lower half of \\(\\texttt{lem:known-propensity-benchmark}\\), followed by (32), yields\n\\[\n c r_{\\mathrm{or}}(n)\\le\\rho_{n,e}^*\\le\\rho_n^*.                    \\tag{33}\n\\]\nCombining (31) and (33) proves the two-sided assertion. For \\(d=\\gamma=1\\), \\(\\alpha=1/10\\), and \\(\\beta=9/10\\), the hypotheses hold with equality in \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\ge\\gamma\\), and \\(2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)=2/5\\), proving the stated example.",
